WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.726
Here I edit the relationship between Shipments and Sales Person.

2
00:00:04.726 --> 00:00:10.220
They connect on the salesperson ID, and the cardinality is many to one.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:19.137
On the report page, slicers for salesperson and date all filter the same Shipments table.

4
00:00:19.137 --> 00:00:22.820
This works because of the model behind the page.

5
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.747
In Model view, Shipments sits in the middle.

6
00:00:36.747 --> 00:00:38.465
It is the fact table.

7
00:00:38.465 --> 00:00:43.273
It holds the numbers we measure, such as the amount and the number of

8
00:00:43.273 --> 00:00:46.020
boxes, and an ID for each related table.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.539
Around it are the lookup tables, also called dimension tables:

10
00:01:04.539 --> 00:01:07.263
Sales Person, Product, Geography, and Calendar.

11
00:01:07.263 --> 00:01:10.440
They describe who, what, where, and when.

12
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.488
This shape is called a star schema.

13
00:01:26.488 --> 00:01:31.465
Every lookup table connects to the fact table with a one to many relationship,

14
00:01:31.465 --> 00:01:35.020
and filters flow from the lookup tables to the facts.

15
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.790
Manage relationships confirms the four links:

16
00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:55.440
Geography, Product, Calendar on the ship date, and Sales Person.

17
00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.300
All four are active.

18
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.673
A star schema keeps your model simple, fast, and easy to explain.

19
00:02:19.673 --> 00:02:25.124
When you design your own reports, aim for one fact table in the middle,

20
00:02:25.124 --> 00:02:27.460
with clean lookup tables around it.

